the upside to the pandemic is in the air  it's cleaner there's less pollution  because we're flying less  driving less and industry has slowed to  almost a halt  greenhouse gas emissions plunged in  april by about 17  overall in some countries the drop was  more like 40 percent  that is great news and it feels like  nature  is rebalancing a bit though as jeff  semple explains it's just a blip in the  big picture of climate change  paul koch woke up one morning last month  to find a trespasser  taking a dip so i called my wife and  said there's a moose in the pool the 800  pounder made quite a splash  for several hours before making her own  way out of the backyard  coke and his wife have lived in this  suburban ottawa neighborhood for 40  years  but this was a first we've had lots of  pool parties over the years but not one  with a moose  or any animal with humans living in  lockdown the animal kingdom  is reclaiming some territory  in thailand these monkeys are going ape  embroiled in a street fight  over food in the center of town  wild turkeys are strolling the suburbs  of louisiana  even penguins have been spotted  jaywalking in south africa  the list of unusual wildlife sightings  goes on and on  and on this cartoon pretty much sums up  the sentiment even here on the campus  the university of toronto scarborough  when i bike in i routinely see deer now  in campus  the major inhibitor of the movement of  animals across the city is traffic  will be busy roads and now we've seen  major roads made arteries in the city  being almost devoid of cars this spring  traffic volumes in toronto were cut  in half and those silent streets are  also providing a breath  of fresh air smoggy skylines from some  of the world's most polluted places  are clearing the contrast is even  starker from space  revealing nitrogen dioxide emissions in  china and italy  before and after the lockdown in india  particle pollution fell by nearly 60  percent  we are seeing improvements here in north  america as well during the pandemics  peak  canada's largest city saw air pollution  levels highlighted  here in blue drop by 15 percent  i would suspect that we could probably  see a 40-50 percent reduction  in the number of smoggy days pollution  kills 7  million people every year according to  the world health organization  and some experts expect that this  cleaner air  might actually save more lives this year  than are lost to the virus  and that plummeting pollution is also  sparking a debate  over whether the pandemic might be  flattening the climate curve  2020 is witnessing a carbon crash  co2 emissions which fuel climate change  are taking a nosedive unlike anything  we've seen before  the reduction in in terms of tons of co2  which is what really matters for the  atmosphere  we're definitely going to have by far  the biggest reduction ever  the good news even as economies reopen  carbon emissions are expected to end the  year  down around 8 the equivalent of india's  entire  energy use which brings us to the bad  news  even if we get an 8 reduction this year  that's that's the sort of  magnitude of reduction we would need  this year  next year the year after and every  single year  in order to limit warming to 1.5 degrees  to meet the paris summit goal and  prevent the worst of climate change  global emissions need to fall by 7.6  every year for a decade even this  unprecedented lockdown  amounts to little more than a first step  basically we need this kind of reduction  every year  and we don't want to have a pandemic to  do that we don't want to have an  economic recession  to do that and so we're going to need to  make relatively large changes  and some believe this moment represents  our best chance for that change  a golden opportunity to go green  we all get up every morning we get into  our cars we drive we  go to meetings all over the world and  everywhere all the time  and now we've learned hello i think we  can do these things a lot better  jatin nathwani's office offers a glimpse  into a sustainable future  a zero-carbon building one of canada's  first with a solar paneled roof and  geothermal heating  the university of waterloo professor  believes this economic crisis  offers a chance to invest in these  technologies  he points to the last time carbon  emissions dropped following the global  recession in 2009  governments then responded by pumping  billions into stimulus projects  construction designed to boost the  economy but that also saw a sharp spike  in emissions  there wasn't anything green about it  because there's typically not much green  about  you know expanding a road or building a  bridge or putting a new  roof on the community ring but this time  around  many countries are eyeing a so-called  green  recovery the european union is making  climate change the centerpiece of its  trillion dollar investment  a stimulus package steering away from  fossil fuels and towards  energy efficient projects france is  offering financial aid to beleaguered  airlines but only in exchange for  investments  in lower emission aircraft even china  the world's largest emitter has endorsed  big spending on a slew of low-carbon  infrastructure  albeit while still building new coal  plants  i believe that this is going to be  different and  will be different partly because the  recognition  the notion that we are really facing  a very severe climate question is no  longer a matter of debate  the united states may prove the  exception as the white house steadily  slashes green protections  but what about canada so far the federal  government has pledged 1.7 billion to  clean up  orphaned and abandoned oil and gas wells  in alberta saskatchewan and bc  salvaging thousands of jobs if we can  put some of the people who were  displaced out of the oil patch back to  work  into the cleanup process then that  actually  is you know is helping in that labor  market canada's oil sector was already  reeling before the pandemic pushed gas  prices down  another 20 percent analysts predict  demand for oil  may take years to fully recover i think  we're seeing a  transformation here that is quite  fundamental that decouples our  dependence on  oil and fossil fuels he argues this  pandemic presents an opportunity to fast  track the transition to renewable energy  we should aim for an orderly phase an  orderly transition  to efficiency electric vehicles  rather than trying to prop up and  maintain an industry that has no  long-term future  the government is being offered an  opportunity  on a golden plat platter but i'm not too  confident that they will take that  opportunity political will to push  drastic change can be hard to come by in  normal times  but this is anything but the pandemic  has seen  millions heed the advice of experts to  protect  against an invisible threat the canadian  public has listened  we've stayed home we physically  distanced  we are listening to medical experts and  their advice  to keep us safe now we need to do the  same thing  when it comes to climate change to  protect the future not only for  ourselves  but also our furry friends
 

